The list feature on a calculator allows you to store, edit, and manipulate an ordered collection (array) of multiple numbers all at once, rather than working with just a single number at a time. 
Other features such as the sum list or the prod list features. These are functions on graphing and scientific calculators that add or multiply all the individual numbers or elements stored in a list at once. We address below how to use this feature using both Texas and Casio calculators.

\begin{solutionbox} \begin{center}Casio fx-9750GII\end{center}\begin{enumerate}[label=\protect\circled{\color{white}\arabic*}]
\item \begin{bf}Step one:\end{bf}   Enter the data: start the calculator and press \keystroke{menu} and the key \keystroke{2} to access the list menu (stat). You will follow all the steps on this menu. Type the List 1 and List 2 values.
 \item \begin{bf}Step two:\end{bf} Calculate List1$\wedge$List2: in the same menu with the arrow key move on top of List 3. Now press the \keystroke{OPTION} key and then the List option (key \keystroke{F1}). Now press the key \keystroke{1} to then pressing the power key \keystroke{$\wedge$} and again the \keystroke{OPTION} key and List now 2. If you press \keystroke{EXE}, List 3 will be populated with the result of List1$\wedge$List2 done row by row.
\item \begin{bf}Step three:\end{bf} Calculate Sum(List1): in the same menu with the arrow key move to the first row of List 4. In the list menu you will see the \keystroke{   $\triangleright$ }. Press this key until you find the \keystroke{   Sum } function. Press it and then again \keystroke{OPTION} and select List1. If you press \keystroke{EXE}, the first row of List 4 will contain the result of adding all elements of List1.
\item \begin{bf}Step four:\end{bf} Calculate Prod(List1): in the same menu with the arrow key move to the first row of List 4. In the list menu you will see the \keystroke{   $\triangleright$ }. Press this key until you find the \keystroke{ Prod} function. Press it and then again \keystroke{OPTION} and select List1. If you press \keystroke{EXE}, the first row of List 4 will contain the result of multipling all elements of List1.
\end{enumerate}
\end{solutionbox}
You need to follow the following steps to use the list features in a TI-84:
\begin{solutionbox} \begin{center}Texas TI-84\end{center}\begin{enumerate}[label=\protect\circled{\color{white}\arabic*}]
\item \begin{bf}Step one:\end{bf} Enter the data: press \keystroke{Stat} and \keystroke{Enter}. Type the List 1 and List 2 values.
 \item \begin{bf}Step two:\end{bf} Calculate L1$\wedge$L2: in the same menu with the arrow key move on top of L3. Now press the \keystroke{=}, \keystroke{second} and \keystroke{1} keys to then pressing \keystroke{$\wedge$} ,\keystroke{second} and \keystroke{2}. If you press \keystroke{EXE}, List 3 will be populated with the result of List1$\wedge$List2 done row by row.
